Q: Every pptx made with OpenXml prompts a repair by PowerPoint A: <p>I think the root cause is two structural issues that OpenXML validators won't catch since they only enforce schema validity, not standard practice.</p>
<p><strong>1. <code>SlideMaster</code> is missing <code>TextStyles</code> (primary cause)</strong></p>
<p>Including <code>TextStyles</code> in every <code>SlideMaster</code> is standard practice. Microsoft's own documentation always includes it (<a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/office/open-xml/presentation/working-with-slide-masters" rel="nofollow noreferrer">https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/office/open-xml/presentation/working-with-slide-masters</a>), and every PowerPoint-generated <code>.pptx</code> has it in <code>slideMaster1.xml</code>. Omitting it keeps the XML schema valid, but PowerPoint will repair the file every time it opens.</p>
<pre class="lang-cs prettyprint-override"><code>masterPart.SlideMaster =
    new SlideMaster(
        new CommonSlideData(...),
        new ColorMap { ... },
        new SlideLayoutIdList(
            new SlideLayoutId { Id = SlideLayoutId, RelationshipId = &quot;rIdLayout&quot; }
        ),
        new TextStyles(        // this was missing
            new TitleStyle(),
            new BodyStyle(),
            new OtherStyle()
        )
    );
</code></pre>
<p><strong>2. Blank layout contains a placeholder shape (secondary cause)</strong></p>
<p><code>BuildSlideLayout</code> adds a <code>Shape</code> with a <code>PlaceholderShape</code> inside it, but the layout is declared as <code>SlideLayoutValues.Blank</code>. A blank layout must not contain placeholder shapes, so remove it entirely from the <code>ShapeTree</code>:</p>
<pre class="lang-cs prettyprint-override"><code>private static void BuildSlideLayout(SlideLayoutPart part)
{
    part.SlideLayout =
        new SlideLayout(
            new CommonSlideData(
                new ShapeTree(
                    new NonVisualGroupShapeProperties(
                        new NonVisualDrawingProperties { Id = 1, Name = &quot;&quot; },
                        new NonVisualGroupShapeDrawingProperties(),
                        new ApplicationNonVisualDrawingProperties()
                    ),
                    new GroupShapeProperties(new OpenXmlDrawing.TransformGroup())
                    // No shape/placeholder for a blank layout
                )
            ),
            new ColorMapOverride(new OpenXmlDrawing.MasterColorMapping())
        )
        { Type = SlideLayoutValues.Blank };

    part.SlideLayout.Save();
}
</code></pre>

Q: StreamSubscription not resuming when the app comes back to foreground A: <p>If the number of times a pause request is sent isn't critical to you, you can use the <code>PauseTransformer</code> transformer and the <code>PauseTokenSource</code> pause mechanism.</p>
<p>An example is given below.</p>
<pre class="lang-dart prettyprint-override"><code>import 'dart:async';

import 'package:multitasking/misc/pause.dart';
import 'package:multitasking/multitasking.dart';

Future&lt;void&gt; main() async {
  await _initStreamAndSubscribe();

  /// Demo
  final watch = Stopwatch();
  watch.start();
  final timer = Timer.periodic(Duration(milliseconds: 100), (timer) {
    print('Time: ${watch.elapsedMilliseconds}');
  });

  // Pause with multiple calls
  Timer(Duration(milliseconds: 150), () async {
    print('Pause');
    await _pts?.pause();
    await _pts?.pause();
    await _pts?.pause();
    await _pts?.pause();
    await _pts?.pause();
  });

  // Resume with one call
  Timer(Duration(milliseconds: 550), () async {
    print('Resume');
    await _pts?.resume();
  });

  await Future&lt;void&gt;.delayed(Duration(milliseconds: 700));
  timer.cancel();
  _subscription?.cancel().ignore();
  print('Cancel');
}

PauseTokenSource? _pts;
StreamSubscription&lt;int&gt;? _subscription;

Future&lt;void&gt; _initStreamAndSubscribe() async {
  _pts = PauseTokenSource();
  final stream = Stream.periodic(Duration(milliseconds: 100), (count) {
    print('Sent: $count');
    return count;
  });

  final controlledStream = stream.asPausable(_pts!.token);
  _subscription = controlledStream.listen((event) {
    print('Received: $event');
  });
}

</code></pre>
<p>Results:</p>
<pre class="lang-none prettyprint-override"><code>Sent: 0
Received: 0
Time: 113
Pause
Time: 201
Time: 302
Time: 402
Time: 501
Resume
Time: 601
Sent: 1
Received: 1
Time: 701
Cancel
</code></pre>
<p>The advantage is that you don't need a subscription for this. That is, a subscription is certainly required, but it's not used to implement pause; you only need the stream.<br />
This way, you can control the iteration of a stream using <code>awit for</code> statement where a subscription is not available.<br />
And you can always interrupt (cancel) the iteration using the <code>CancellationTransformer</code> transformer.</p>
